!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/fixtures/validate-alias.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/36/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/36/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  A type annotation written as an alias. [TSON-SCHEMA] §7.2 compares a value's annotation against the
  position's type "after reference flattening of both", so an alias and its target are one type at either
  end of that comparison — at the position's own type and at each of its subtypes alike.

  `s_of` is the ordinary case: a REFERENCE entry naming a subtype, written where the subtype's own name
  would be. An implementation that flattens only the position's side refuses it.

  `ok_of` is the case that cannot be written any other way. `ok<text>` closes to an entry the resolver
  minted, whose name is implementation-chosen and non-normative (§8.2) — so no document can name it, and an
  alias is the only spelling there is. A processor that does not flatten the subtype side cannot admit any
  member of a subtype-template family at all.

  `apart` is unrelated to `base` and `a_of` names it: flattening decides what a name means, never whether
  it is admitted, so this one stays refused.
  """
{
  base => {
    tag: text
  }

  sub => base & {
    extra: text
  }

  s_of => sub

  apart => {
    only: text
  }

  a_of => apart

  result => <T> {
    payload: T
  }

  ok => <T> result<T> & {
    note: text
  }

  result_of => result<text>
  ok_of     => ok<text>

  holder => {
    b: base
    r?: result_of?
  }
}
